Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulations are fundamental computational tools for investigating the physical behavior of materials at the atomic and molecular scales. The performance of existing MD packages remains limited by the costs of neighbor list construction, irregular memory access, and synchronization overheads. This paper presents OCTANE, a GPU-native MD framework that employs an octree-based spatial index to eliminate explicit neighbor list construction and enhance data access efficiency. OCTANE integrates constant-time bottom-up neighbor search and tree update algorithms with GPU optimizations towards high spatial locality and memory coalescing. It also parallelizes most computations, minimizing atomic operations and synchronization costs. The framework supports Berendsen and Nosé–Hoover thermostats, records thermodynamic properties, and generates OVITO-compatible trajectories. Validation using the Lennard-Jones interatomic potential for Argon systems shows that OCTANE achieves up to 16 × higher performance and ∼ 7 × lower GPU memory usage than LAMMPS on an NVIDIA H100 GPU, and up to 2.5 × speedup with ∼ 8 × memory savings on a TITAN V GPU. While LAMMPS fails to scale to 10-million-atom systems on the TITAN V due to memory limitations, OCTANE successfully executes these simulations. These results demonstrate OCTANE’s scalability and efficiency for large-scale GPU-accelerated molecular dynamics simulations. OCTANE also includes a fully GPU-parallel radial distribution function (RDF) computation, enabling efficient structural analysis without introducing additional synchronization overhead.